// expect-error: NITPICK-RESOLVE-001
// expect-error: NITPICK-RESOLVE-001
//
// A NAME DECLARED TWICE INSIDE A TYPE. Nothing here involves a macro: the rule is
// about structs and enums, and it is in this suite rather than the expansion one
// for that reason. Splicing (0.6.3) is what made the collision easy to produce by
// accident — `struct:P = { #xy(); #xy(); };` — and a check that fired only on
// spliced members would make a construct mean something different depending on
// where it came from.
//
// THE SECOND ONE IS UNREACHABLE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS AN ERROR AND NOT A WARNING.
// Field lookup returns the FIRST match and gets no say in which was meant, while
// layout gives both storage — so the struct is the size of two fields, one of them
// permanently unwritable and unreadable, and nothing anywhere says so.
mod:duplicate_members;

struct:P = {
    int32:x;
    int32:x;
};

enum:E = {
    Alpha;
    Alpha;
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) { exit 0i32; };
